    1. LOCATION FIRST. If the gene lies on the X chromosome (outside the pseudoautosomal regions), inheritance_mode is "X-linked" for EVERY association of that gene, regardless of the proband's zygosity: hemizygous males, heterozygous females (X-linked dominant or skewed X-inactivation), de novo, or inherited. A single heterozygous or de novo allele in an X-linked gene is "X-linked", NOT "Monoallelic". "Monoallelic" and "Biallelic" are AUTOSOMAL-ONLY labels — never apply them to an X-chromosome gene, never record "Monoallelic"/"Biallelic" while relegating "X-linked" to inheritance_details, and never split an X-linked gene into separate "Monoallelic" and "X-linked" associations. If a contributing paper calls an X-linked gene's variant "heterozygous", "de novo", or "monoallelic", still classify the association "X-linked". (Pseudoautosomal genes such as SHOX inherit autosomally — treat them as autosomal.)
    2. AUTOSOMAL → USE ZYGOSITY. For autosomal genes, determine Monoallelic vs Biallelic from zygosity evidence in the papers (e.g., "heterozygous", "de novo" → Monoallelic; "homozygous", "compound heterozygous" → Biallelic), NOT from variant type. Loss-of-function variants (nonsense, frameshift, splice-site) can cause EITHER monoallelic (haploinsufficiency) OR biallelic (recessive) disease. When the same phenotype has evidence for BOTH monoallelic and biallelic inheritance, create TWO associations (see STEP 1 above).
    MOSAIC/SOMATIC VARIANTS: Mosaicism describes the cellular distribution of a variant, not its zygosity — both monoallelic and biallelic mosaic states exist (e.g., a constitutive heterozygous variant combined with post-zygotic loss-of-heterozygosity on the second allele yields a biallelic mosaic state, though this is uncommon outside cancer). The location-first rule still governs: a mosaic variant in an X-linked gene is "X-linked". For autosomal genes, classify by the zygosity reported in the contributing papers, and record the mosaic context in `inheritance_details` as "mosaic":
      - Single mosaic variant with no second pathogenic variant reported on the partner allele → "Monoallelic"
      - Compound heterozygous mosaic, homozygous mosaic, or constitutive heterozygous combined with post-zygotic LOH on the second allele → "Biallelic"
      - Use "Other" ONLY when the contributing papers themselves leave zygosity genuinely ambiguous (e.g., describe a mosaic finding without indicating whether the partner allele also carries a pathogenic variant). A paper's labelling conventions are signals: a paper that explicitly tags biallelic mosaic cases (e.g., "homozygous mosaic", "Mosaic AR") but uses a bare label such as "Mosaic" for a given case is reporting monoallelic mosaicism — classify accordingly rather than defaulting to "Other".
